## wt — Cloud Workspace Workflow

`wt` (`~/.local/bin/wt`) is the CLI for developing the Snowflake monorepo on
**`sf ws` cloud workspaces**. Edit locally in a (sparse) git worktree; build
and deploy on a remote cloud workspace. It is a single local bash script — nothing
to install on the workspace.

**Model — "reset main in place":** code is transferred **peer-to-peer over SSH**
(only the git delta crosses the wire). `wt deploy` resets the workspace clone at
`/home/repo/snowflake` (detached) to your synced commit — your code is instantly live for
`sf sut` — and `wt deactivate` restores it to `main`. No bind mounts, symlinks, or sudo on
either side. One workspace builds one feature at a time, guarded by a **lock file**
(`/home/repo/.wt.lock`) that records which local worktree owns the checkout.

### Layout

| Location | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `~/Projects/snowflake` | Read-only main clone (`WT_LOCAL_MAIN`; never edited) |
| `~/Projects/snowflake-worktree/worktree-<name>/` | Local worktree (`WT_LOCAL_ROOT`) |
| `<worktree>/.git/.../wt.conf` | Per-worktree state (bound workspace + remote clone path) |
| `<ws>:/home/repo/snowflake` | Workspace clone (`WT_REMOTE_MAIN`) — push target, reset target; where `sf sut` runs |
| `<ws>:/home/repo/.wt.lock` | Owner lock — `user@host:/local/worktree/path`; empty when idle on `main` |
| `<ws>:/home/repo/.wt.sut-pending` | Internal marker: stop SUT once on first build after a fresh claim |
| `<ws>` tmux session `wt` | Durable session that runs `sf sut …` |

### Configuration

Precedence: **ambient env var > config file > built-in default**.
- Config file: `~/.config/wt/config` (override path with `$WT_CONFIG`), a sourced shell snippet.
- Variables: `WT_LOCAL_MAIN`, `WT_LOCAL_ROOT`, `WT_REMOTE_MAIN`, `WT_REMOTE_LOCK`.
- `wt config` prints resolved values; `wt config --init` scaffolds the file pre-filled.
- One-off: `WT_LOCAL_ROOT=~/wt wt create foo`.

### Command reference

```bash
wt ls                         # list sf workspaces; marks this worktree's bound target
wt create <name> [--base <b>] [--sparse <path>...]        # NEW branch as a (sparse) worktree
wt checkout <remote-branch> [--name <dir>] [--sparse ...] # worktree from an EXISTING origin branch (alias: co)
wt use [<workspace>|--auto]   # bind this worktree to a workspace (no name / --auto = pick a free RUNNING one); starts it if stopped
wt deploy [--no-clean] [--force]      # sync this worktree (incl. uncommitted+untracked) onto the workspace
wt build|redeploy|setup [--force] [--attach]  # deploy, then sf sut … (detached tmux; --attach to follow live)
wt attach                     # attach to the running build's remote tmux session
wt ssh [cmd...]               # shell/command at the build path
wt status                     # local dirty state · workspace HEAD · active worktree (lock owner) · GS up/down (snow ps)
wt deactivate [--force]       # restore the workspace clone to 'main' and release the lock
wt sync                       # fetch + fast-forward the local main clone
wt config [--init]            # show (or scaffold) the resolved local/remote paths
wt remove [--force]           # remove the local worktree + its mapping (workspace untouched)
```

### Typical workflows

```bash
# Fresh feature on a new branch (sparse to a subtree of the huge repo)
wt sync                                   # keep local main current first
wt create SNOW-1234-fix --sparse GlobalServices
cd ~/Projects/snowflake-worktree/worktree-SNOW-1234-fix
wt use alice                              # bind to a running workspace
# ...edit locally (uncommitted is fine)...
wt build                                  # sync + sf sut build, in durable tmux

# Work on an existing remote branch
wt checkout someones-feature-branch --sparse GlobalServices
cd ~/Projects/snowflake-worktree/worktree-someones-feature-branch
wt use bob && wt redeploy

# Iterate; reconnect after a dropped link; finish
wt redeploy                               # each deploy ships only the new delta
wt attach                                 # re-attach to the build if SSH dropped
wt deactivate                             # restore the workspace to main, release the lock
wt remove --force                         # drop the local worktree when done
```

### Building & testing (remote only — wt never builds locally)
Code can only be built, deployed, and tested on the workspace. After `wt deploy`:
- **GS ITs, GS unit, XP unit tests:** run them on the workspace with **bazel**, over `wt ssh`
  (run long suites inside a tmux/zellij session so they survive SSH drops). `wt build` compiles
  via `sf sut build`.
- **Snowfort tests:** deploy the worktree first, then follow the **Snowfort skill** in the
  repo's `Snowfort/` directory — it documents how to run them.

### Key rules
- Until a code change is needed you may work on `main` in the read-only main clone; the moment
  you must edit code, create a sparse worktree (`wt create` / `wt checkout --sparse <paths>`) and
  drive the workspace with `wt` — never build/test against the shared main clone.
- Commands act on the worktree containing your **current directory**; pass `-w/--worktree <name>`
  (any position; bare name, `worktree-<name>` dir, or path) to target a worktree by name without
  `cd`-ing into it — e.g. `wt -w SNOW-1234-fix build`.
- Never edit `~/Projects/snowflake` (the main clone) directly — always work in a worktree
  (`wt create`/`checkout`). Revert accidental edits to the main clone with `git restore .`
  (not `git checkout -- <files>`). Run `wt sync` before `wt create` so new branches
  start from current `main` (it drifts thousands of commits/day).
- **Choosing a remote — free + load-balanced.** `wt use` with no name (or `wt use
  --auto`) auto-selects among RUNNING workspaces that are free (lock empty or already owned by
  this worktree), picking the one with the **fewest local worktrees bound to it** so work
  spreads across machines instead of piling onto one. Pass an explicit name to force a specific
  workspace. If none are free, it lists the owners.
- `deploy`/`build`/`redeploy`/`setup` capture **uncommitted and untracked** work (gitignored
  artifacts excluded) — no need to commit before building.
- Sparsity is **local only**; the workspace always materializes the full tree to build.
- A workspace is **claimed** by the first worktree that deploys to it (owner stored in
  `.wt.lock`). Another worktree's deploy is refused with the owner shown — release with
  `wt deactivate` or override with `--force`. One SUT per workspace.
- `build`/`redeploy`/`setup` run inside a remote **tmux** session named `wt` (detached by default — use `--attach` or `wt attach` to follow live); they
  survive SSH drops.
- `wt` runs `sf sut stop` once per activation — on the **first** `build`/`redeploy`/`setup`
  after a worktree claims a workspace, and on `deactivate`. Plain `wt deploy` never stops the
  SUT (it must not disrupt a SUT someone may be using via the UI); reruns don't re-stop.
- `wt deploy` runs `git clean -fd` on the workspace clone for fidelity (keeps gitignored
  build artifacts); use `--no-clean` if you keep scratch files directly in the workspace clone.
- Git's global `core.sshCommand` (`sf __ssh`) cannot reach workspace hosts; `wt` forces
  plain `ssh` for the delta push — so workspaces must be reachable as `ssh <workspace-name>`
  (sf writes the ssh config). Run `sf ws ls` to see status; only `RUNNING` ones are usable.

