## PR Format: snowflake-eng/snowflake

### Title
```
SNOW-XXXXXXX: Brief description of the change
```

### Body template
```markdown
## Summary

- <bullet: what changed and why>
- <bullet: additional notable changes>

Tracked under SNOW-XXXXXXX. (include for subtasks; omit otherwise)

## Test plan

- [x] `<build/test command>` — <result or link>
- [x] This change has passed precommit: <buildkite URL>
- [x] This change is protected by parameter — <details, or "N/A, removing parameter" / "N/A, non-behavioral change">
```

Notes:
- Use `## Context` instead of `## Summary` when extra background is needed (e.g., explaining why a parameter is being removed after rollout).
- The precommit checkbox line is required; link to the Buildkite build.
- The "protected by parameter" checkbox is required; mark N/A if the change is not behind a flag.
- To start a new precommit, run: `sf ci build start default-precommit`
- ALWAYS include the Jira ticket number in the PR title (e.g. "SNOW-12345: ...")
- If no Jira number is specified, ASK the user before committing/pushing
- Never create a PR without a Jira number in the header

## Precommit discipline

**Precommits are expensive. NEVER start a precommit after each small fix.**

The correct iteration loop is:
1. Make all code changes locally in the worktree
2. Sync to the workspace: `wt deploy` (pushes the delta, resets the workspace clone in place)
3. Run tests on the remote SUT — use bazel (GS UTs/ITs) or the Snowfort regression CLI to validate (`wt build`/`redeploy` to (re)build first, `wt ssh` to run them)
4. Fix any failures by going back to step 1
5. Only when all tests pass on the remote SUT, start a precommit: `sf ci build start default-precommit`

**Do NOT start a precommit to discover test failures.** Test locally on the SUT first. Precommit is the final gate before the PR is ready, not a debugging tool.
